---
description: Safe Django migration practices for PostgreSQL.
globs:
  - "**/migrations/*.py"
  - "src/apps/**/models.py"
  - "src/apps/**/models/**/*.py"
alwaysApply: false
---

# Database migration safety

- Never hand-edit an already deployed migration. Add a new migration.
- Review generated operations and PostgreSQL SQL before applying or committing.
- Separate schema introduction, data backfill, behavior switch, constraint validation, and destructive cleanup when deployment compatibility requires it.
- New required columns on populated tables normally use: nullable/additive schema -> batched backfill -> application switch -> constraint/default cleanup.
- Avoid table rewrites, long blocking locks, and unbounded data migrations.
- Make data migrations forwards-safe, resumable or idempotent where practical, and explicit about reverse behavior.
- Do not import current model classes in data migrations; use historical models from `apps.get_model`.
- Add indexes deliberately and assess write/read tradeoffs. Use concurrent PostgreSQL index operations when production size and deployment mechanics require them.
- New uniqueness and check constraints need handling for existing data and race-condition tests.
- Destructive operations require an accepted ADR/feature spec, verified backup/recovery posture, and a rollback or forward-fix plan.
- `makemigrations --check --dry-run` and migration tests must pass in CI.
